544921875, -0.0244598388671875, -0.0257720947265625, -0.01200103759765625, -0.01079559326171875, 0.0044403076171875, 0.02435302734375, 0.002590179443359375, -0.003559112548828125, 0.007106781005859375, -0.005481719970703125, -0.002674102783203125, 0.0255126953125, -0.000335693359375, 0.037933349609375, 0.00508880615234375, 0.022979736328125, 0.0004744529724121094, 0.037933349609375, 0.01861572265625, 0.006511688232421875, 0.0169830322265625, -0.01198577880859375, -0.0165863037109375, 0.0137176513671875, -0.018310546875, -0.02630615234375, 0.02813720703125, 0.005859375, 0.00959014892578125, -0.033966064453125, -0.01070404052734375, -0.0137786865234375, -0.00040793418884277344, 0.037994384765625, -0.019287109375, 0.006046295166015625, 0.00321197509765625, -0.022308349609375, 0.020050048828125, -0.0196990966796875, -0.0258331298828125, 0.01385498046875, 0.00464630126953125, 0.0103912353515625, 0.01416015625, 0.0235137939453125, 0.0225677490234375, -0.005126953125, -0.00531005859375, -0.0129852294921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods\n\nStudy Design and Dataset  \nThis retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI pipeline was developed for report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three sequential agents, each with distinct and non-overlapping functions:\n\n| Agent Name         | Primary Input                | Output                                  | Error Types Addressed                          |\n|--------------------|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|\n| Sectioning Agent   | Raw report text             | Sentences assigned to anatomical sections| Section assignment errors (mismatches, omissions, duplications) |\n| Consistency Agent  | Sectioned report            | List of flagged cross-section inconsistencies | Discrepancies between Findings and Impression (e.g., mismatched laterality, organ, or critical findings) |\n| QA Agent           | Sectioned report, flagged findings | Flags for gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented critical findings | Gender-anatomy conflicts, missing documentation of critical finding communication |\n\n**Sectioning Agent**: Assigns sentences to anatomical sections using a combination of rule-based (regular expressions) and large language model (LLM; Llama-2-13B, default parameters, no fine-tuning) methods. Sentences with unambiguous section references are assigned via rules; ambiguous cases are resolved by the LLM using sentence context. For example, the regex `r\"\\b(liver|hepatic)\\b\"` is used to assign sentences mentioning \"liver\" or \"hepatic\" to the Liver section. For ambiguous sentences, the LLM is prompted with:  \n*\"Assign the following sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section (e.g., Liver, Lung, Kidney): '[sentence]'. Respond with only the section name.\"*  \nLLM outputs were audited during development, and systematic errors led to prompt refinement or manual assignment.\n\n**Consistency Agent**: Checks for cross-section consistency of key findings. It identifies key clinical findings using a curated term list (e.g., \"nodule,\" \"mass,\" \"effusion\") and flags discrepancies between Findings and Impression sections, such as mismatches in laterality, organ, or critical findings. For example, if \"left renal mass\" appears in Findings but not in Impression, a discrepancy is flagged. Rules and term lists were refined using a development set of 50 reports.\n\n**QA Agent**: Flags gender-anatomy conflicts and checks for documentation of communication regarding critical findings. Gender-anatomy conflicts are detected via keyword matching (e.g., \"prostate\" in a report for a female patient) and LLM-based contextual analysis. For critical findings, the agent checks for documentation of communication (e.g., presence of phrases like \"results discussed with referring physician\"). LLM outputs were reviewed and prompt rules refined as needed.\n\nTechnical implementation details, including additional regex patterns and LLM prompt engineering, are provided in the Supplementary Materials. The above examples illustrate the approach used in the main text.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection  \nA subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling ensured proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports, based on their prevalence in the full dataset. The subset size was determined to ensure at least 10 examples of each error type, based on their observed prevalence (section mismatches ~13%, gender-anatomy conflicts ~1%, undocumented critical findings ~0.5%), while maintaining feasibility for manual review. No formal power calculation was performed due to the exploratory nature of the study and the rarity of some error types, but the sample size was chosen to maximize inclusion of rare errors and provide sufficient examples for qualitative assessment. Limitations include reduced statistical power and generalizability due to the small sample size.\n\nEvaluation Metrics\n\n**Operational Definitions**\n- *Correctly restructured*: The report is segmented so each sentence is assigned to the appropriate anatomical section, with no alteration of clinical content or meaning. Sentences about a particular structure (e.g., \"hepatic steatosis\") appear only in the corresponding section (e.g., \"Liver\"), with no omissions, duplications, or misplacements.\n- *Incorrectly restructured*: Errors in section assignment (e.g., a lung finding in the \"Liver\" section), omitted sentences, or altered meaning (e.g., splitting a sentence so clinical context is lost). For findings relevant to multiple organs, assignment to either relevant section is acceptable; omission or assignment to an unrelated section is incorrect.\n\n**Reviewer Rubric**\n- All sentences must be present and assigned to the correct anatomical section.\n- No sentences should be assigned to multiple sections unless clinically justified (e.g., findings spanning two organs).\n- The clinical meaning of each sentence must be preserved.\n- For ambiguous cases (e.g., \"There is a mass at the hepatic-renal interface\"), assignment to either the Liver or Kidney section is acceptable, but omission or assignment to an unrelated section (e.g., Lung) is incorrect.\n- If a sentence describes a finding relevant to multiple sections (e.g., \"There is a large mass involving both the liver and right kidney\"), assignment to both sections is acceptable if the sentence is not duplicated or altered.\n- Any deviation from these criteria constitutes an incorrect restructuring.\n\n**Examples**\n- \"There is a right lower lobe nodule\" in the \"Liver\" section: incorrect.\n- \"Mild hepatic steatosis\" in the \"Liver\" section: correct.\n- \"There is a mass at the hepatic-renal interface\": assignment to either \"Liver\" or \"Kidney\" section is acceptable.\n- \"There is a large mass involving both the liver and right kidney\": assignment to both \"Liver\" and \"Kidney\" sections is acceptable if not duplicated or altered.\n\n**QA Performance Ratings**\n- *Excellent*: All relevant issues correctly flagged, no errors or omissions.\n- *Good*: Minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation (e.g., missed typographical inconsistency).\n- *Fair*: Errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation (e.g., missing a critical finding or misclassifying a major discrepancy).\n\nReviewers received standardized instructions and a training session with example cases. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nReviewer Disagreement Handling  \nDisagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. If consensus could not be reached, a third board-certified radiologist adjudicated. Consensus ratings were used in all analyses.\n\nStatistical Analysis  \nReviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. For QA flagging, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated for each error type (section mismatch, gender-anatomy conflict, undocumented critical finding) using reviewer consensus as the reference standard. The primary outcome was the proportion of reports correctly restructured by the system. Secondary outcomes included accuracy of QA flagging, precision, recall, F1-score (both overall and by error type), and inter-rater agreement. Missing or ambiguous cases were excluded from analysis and reported separately. All statistical analyses were performed using Python (v3.10) with the `scikit-learn` and `statsmodels` packages.\n\nBaseline/Comparator Data  \nManual structuring and QA performance without the AI system were not formally measured in this study. However, prior internal audits of manual reporting, conducted in 2022 using a similar rubric (section assignment accuracy, documentation of critical findings, and gender-anatomy consistency), involved random sampling of 100 reports reviewed independently by two radiologists with consensus adjudication. These audits identified sectioning errors in approximately 10\u201315% of reports and QA documentation omissions in 5\u20138%, providing a descriptive baseline for contextualization. The definitions and reviewer criteria in these audits were aligned with those used in the current study.\n\nResults\n\n**System Performance Summary**  \nThe multi-agent AI system processed 638 reports, structuring a total of 22,270 sentences. QA issues were flagged in 90 reports (14.1%) in the full dataset. In the evaluation subset (n=45), 15 reports (33.3%) were flagged for at least one QA issue, reflecting the stratified sampling approach.\n\n| Metric                                      | Full Dataset (n=638) | Evaluation Subset (n=45) |\n|---------------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------------|\n| Total sentences structured                  | 22,270               | 1,570                    |\n| Reports flagged for \u22651 QA issue             | 90 (14.1%)           | 15 (33.3%)               |\n| - Section mismatches                        | 80 (12.5%)           | 13 (28.9%)               |\n| - Gender-anatomy conflicts                  | 7 (1.1%)             | 2 (4.4%)                 |\n| - Undocumented critical findings            | 3 (0.5%)             | 1 (2.2%)                 |\n\n**Evaluation Subset Results**  \n- Proportion of correctly restructured reports (by reviewer consensus): 42/45 (93.3%; 95% CI: 82.1\u201398.6%)\n- QA performance ratings: Excellent in 36/45 (80.0%), Good in 7/45 (15.6%), Fair in 2/45 (4.4%)\n- Precision, recall, and F1-score for QA flagging by error type (evaluation subset, n=45):\n\n| Error Type                  | Precision | Recall | F1-score |\n|-----------------------------|-----------|--------|----------|\n| Section mismatch            |"}}